WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.727
This is Clock It, our prototype for IKEA's challenge

00:00:03.777 --> 00:00:07.700
on a fair and just transition across the value chain,

00:00:08.200 --> 00:00:11.300
focused on supplier factories in Vietnam.

00:00:12.600 --> 00:00:14.795
Problems like excessive overtime,

00:00:14.845 --> 00:00:19.200
unsafe conditions or delayed wages can build up between audits.

00:00:19.900 --> 00:00:24.300
Workers are the first to know, but raising concerns through management is hard.

00:00:25.000 --> 00:00:31.900
So our idea is simple: ask workers directly, at a moment they already go through every day.

00:00:33.800 --> 00:00:40.500
At the end of a shift, the worker clocks out on the factory's existing fingerprint scanner.

00:00:41.500 --> 00:00:46.400
Right next to it, a small tablet asks one optional question: how was your shift today?

00:00:47.100 --> 00:00:49.300
The answer is never linked to the fingerprint.

00:00:50.600 --> 00:00:54.400
If the shift went well, it takes one tap.

00:00:55.600 --> 00:00:58.700
The answer is saved, and the worker can head home.

00:01:01.500 --> 00:01:06.800
If the shift was okay or difficult, a few short yes-or-no questions appear:

00:01:07.900 --> 00:01:13.200
overtime, safety, pay, fair treatment, and anything else.

00:01:14.100 --> 00:01:17.800
Here, our worker flags too much overtime and adds a short note.

00:01:18.600 --> 00:01:24.400
Every question can be left blank, and the screen reminds workers not to include names.

00:01:25.800 --> 00:01:31.300
Large buttons and plain language keep it to about a minute. Then one tap to submit.

00:01:32.700 --> 00:01:35.900
And anyone who doesn't want to answer can simply skip.

00:01:38.300 --> 00:01:39.600
Nothing is saved.

00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:45.630
On IKEA's side, our primary user is Maria,

00:01:45.680 --> 00:01:50.500
the Sustainability and Compliance Manager for Vietnam.

00:01:53.000 --> 00:01:56.400
Her dashboard brings the feedback together:

00:01:56.900 --> 00:02:00.901
how many responses came in, the participation rate,

00:02:00.951 --> 00:02:05.400
open signals, and how quickly cases get a first review.

00:02:06.200 --> 00:02:11.000
She can follow how shift ratings change week by week.

00:02:12.600 --> 00:02:15.792
Since ratings only reflect those who chose to answer,

00:02:15.842 --> 00:02:18.900
the dashboard reminds her to read them with care.

00:02:21.600 --> 00:00:24.650
Concerns are grouped into categories,

00:00:24.700 --> 00:02:31.300
so she can filter by working hours or safety and see which issues keep coming back.

00:02:32.400 --> 00:02:36.900
Recurring patterns become cases in a follow-up queue.

00:02:38.200 --> 00:02:40.700
Opening a case shows the next steps:

00:02:41.200 --> 00:02:44.550
check the pattern with worker-informed context,

00:02:44.600 --> 00:02:50.200
choose a safe way to respond, and record whether the action actually helped.

00:02:50.900 --> 00:02:55.350
To be clear about what's real: the worker flow works today

00:02:55.400 --> 00:03:01.500
and stores answers only in the browser, while the dashboard shows example data.

00:03:04.200 --> 00:03:07.950
Next, we want a Vietnamese version with audio guidance

00:03:08.000 --> 00:03:11.000
and a small pilot in one or two factories.

00:03:12.600 --> 00:03:17.200
Clock It: giving workers a voice, and IKEA earlier signals to act on.
